<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Courteen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1572">1572</a>–<a href="/years/1636">1636</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Courteen was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> merchant of Flemish origin who financed the first English settlement of Barbados in 1627. His syndicate claimed the island under the patronage of the Earl of Pembroke but lost it to the rival claim of the Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a>, who received a royal patent. Courteen also backed trading ventures to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>, and his heirs were left heavily in debt.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William Courteen was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1572, the son of William Courteen and Margaret Casiere. His father, the son of a tailor of Menin and a Protestant, had escaped persecution at the hands of the Spaniards to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1568 and with his family set up a manufactory of French hoods in Abchurch Lane, later trading in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and linen in Pudding Lane. As a young man Courteen was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a> as the firm's factor, and there married the daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> merchant Peter Cromling, who brought him a dowry of £60,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> about 1600, and in 1606 he and his brother Peter went into partnership with their brother-in-law John Moncy, William supplying half the capital. In 1619 the Star Chamber fined him £20,000 for exporting gold, but the firm of Courteen &amp; Moncy prospered, its capital estimated at £150,000 in 1631. He was knighted on 31 May 1622. He built ships and traded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>, with a fleet that at one time numbered twenty vessels.</p><p>About 1624 one of his ships found the uninhabited island he named Barbadoes. Letters patent of 25 February 1627-8, addressed to the Earl of Pembroke in trust for Courteen, legalised the colony, and he sent two ships with 1850 people under Captain Powel, who became governor. James Hay, 1st Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a>, claimed all the Caribbee islands under deeds of 1627 and 1628, and in 1629 his representative Henry Hawley imprisoned Powel and took the island. Courteen claimed to have lost £44,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> seizures of his ships in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon">Amboyna</a> massacre, the death of his brother in 1631 and the seizure of the firm's Dutch property by his nephew Peter Boudean weakened his credit, though he remained enormously wealthy. In 1635 Charles I licensed the Courteen association to trade in the East wherever the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> had no presence, but its two ships for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the Dragon and the Katherine, never arrived. He died at the end of May or beginning of June 1636.</p><p>His loans to the crown, made with Sir Paul Pindar and amounting to £200,000, were never repaid. His son William went bankrupt in 1643 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> seized his ships and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1655, and the family's claims to Barbados and to compensation failed. Hans Sloane later acquired the cabinet of curiosities of Courteen's grandson.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Courten” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>English settlers financed by the merchant <b>William Courteen</b> landed at Holetown in 1627 on the uninhabited island.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-barbados">Colony of Barbados</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1613" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William Courteen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1613">What was here in 1613? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
